```java
import java.sql.*;

public class Secure {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/mydb";
        String user = "root";
        String password = "password";
        String query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ? AND password = ?";

        try (Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, password);
             PreparedStatement stmt = conn.prepareStatement(query)) {

            stmt.setString(1, user);
            stmt.setString(2, password);

            try ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery()) {
                while (rs.next()) {
                    System.out.println(rs.getString("username"));
                }
            }
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

In the bad code snippet, the code is vulnerable to SQL injection attacks because the user input is directly concatenated into the SQL query string. This can allow an attacker to execute arbitrary SQL commands by manipulating the user input.

In the good code snippet, the code uses a PreparedStatement to execute the SQL query. The user input is passed as parameters to the PreparedStatement, which automatically escapes any special characters and prevents SQL injection attacks. This is a more secure way to interact with a database.
